</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Being trained on large and diverse datasets, visual foundation models (VFMs) can be fine-tuned to achieve remarkable performance and efficiency in various downstream computer vision tasks. The high computational cost of data collection and training makes these models valuable assets, which motivates some VFM owners to distribute them alongside a license to protect their intellectual property rights. In this paper, we propose an approach to ownership verification of visual foundation models that leverages a small encoder-decoder network to embed digital watermarks into an internal representation of a hold-out set of input images. The method is based on random watermark embedding, which makes the watermark statistics detectable in functional copies of the watermarked model. Both theoretically and experimentally, we demonstrate that the proposed method yields a low probability of false detection for non-watermarked models and a low probability of false misdetection for watermarked models.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Watermarking Visual Foundation Models Fingerprinting
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Today, foundation models are deployed in different fields, for example, in natural language processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, and biology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Their impressive performance in a wide range of downstream tasks comes at a price of high cost of data collection, training, and maintenance. Consequently, the models become valuable assets of their owners: the user’s access to foundation models is mainly organized via subscription to a service where the model is deployed or via purchasing the license to use a specific instance of the model. Unfortunately, some users may violate the terms of use (for example, by integrating their instances of the models into other services to make a profit). Hence, it is reasonable that the models’ owners are willing to defend their intellectual property from unauthorized usage by third parties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One of the prominent approaches to protecting the intellectual property rights (IPRs) of models is watermarking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, the set of methods that embed specific information into a model by modifying its parameters. In watermarking, ownership verification is performed by checking for the presence of this information in a model. An alternative set of methods for IPR protection is based on fingerprinting, which typically does not alter the original model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Instead, these methods generate a unique identifier, or fingerprint, for the model; ownership verification is then conducted by comparing the fingerprint of the original model with that of the suspicious model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This work introduces a method for watermarking visual foundation models (VFMs) by embedding digital watermarks into the hidden representations of a specific set of input images. Within the framework, we experimentally verify that embedding a watermark into the representation allows us to protect the ownership of VFMs fine-tuned for different practical tasks, such as image classification and segmentation. We demonstrate that our approach is able to distinguish between an independent model and functional copies of the watermarked model with high probability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose RandMark, a novel methodology for watermarking visual foundation models. Unlike prior art focused on classifiers, our approach embeds binary signatures directly into the model’s hidden representations via a set of trigger images, making it suitable for the diverse downstream use-cases of VFMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We theoretically derive an upper bound on the probabilities of false positive detection of a non-watermarked model and misdetection of a functional copy of the watermarked model.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Through experiments on state-of-the-art visual foundation models (CLIP and DINOv2), we demonstrate that RandMark is highly robust. It successfully detects model ownership after various functional perturbations, including fine-tuning on downstream tasks (classification and segmentation) and unstructured pruning, where existing fingerprinting methods fail.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10695v1/images/randmark_method_2.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/136;" width="476" height="136" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of the proposed RandMark watermarking pipeline. A binary message is embedded into a visual foundation model using a set of trigger images and an encoder. During verification, randomized input transformations are applied to the trigger set, and a decoder extracts the watermark message from the model outputs. The extracted messages are then compared with the original watermark to verify model ownership.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Visual Foundation Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Visual foundation models, particularly those using vision transformers (ViT, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>), are widely used in modern computer vision due to their scalability and transferability across tasks. The advancement of self-supervised learning methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> has facilitated the creation of general-purpose models, including SimCLR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, and DINOv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. These models learn representations from unlabeled images and demonstrate broad applicability across diverse tasks, often requiring minimal labeled data for fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Protecting Intellectual Property of Neural Networks</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The protection of intellectual property for visual foundation models (VFMs) has gained increasing attention within the field of trustworthy AI. Watermarking and fingerprinting techniques aim to verify model ownership and prevent unauthorized usage or model extraction. While early works focused on large language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, recent efforts adapt these ideas to visual models, including image classifiers and foundation models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For visual foundation models (VFMs), there are currently no watermarking approaches specifically designed for these architectures. Several existing model ownership verification methods, such as ADV-TRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, and IPGuard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, have been proposed in the context of image classification. These approaches embed ownership signatures by either modifying the training objective or introducing crafted input patterns and then detect them based on the model’s responses. While these methods are effective for classification models, they are not directly tailored to the broader capabilities of VFMs, such as image feature extraction or downstream adaptation. Adapting watermarking techniques to visual foundation models thus remains an open challenge and motivates the work presented in this paper. Other complementary methods exploit weight-space smoothing or perturbations to embed ownership information directly into model parameters. For example, Bansal et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> propose model watermarking through weight smoothing in deep neural networks, making the watermark robust to fine-tuning or minor architectural changes. These approaches provide alternative mechanisms to mark models without relying on specific input-output triggers and are particularly relevant for large visual foundation models where modifying the backbone is costly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, while watermarking for VFMs is still in early stages, these methods illustrate that both data-driven triggers and weight-space techniques can serve as practical IP protection strategies for high-capacity visual models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem Statement</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we focus on the problem of watermarking of visual foundation models. To describe the proposed method, we start by introducing the notations. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> be the dimension of the input image and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{s}\to\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{s}\to\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the source VFM that maps input images to the embeddings of dimension <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Here and below, we will write <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}\sim f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}\sim f</annotation></semantics></math> to indicate that the model <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a functional copy of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> that is obtained, for example, via fine-tuning, knowledge distillation or pruning of the original model. Analogously, by writing <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\perp f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⟂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\perp f</annotation></semantics></math> we will indicate that two models, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, are independent of each other. In our method, we train two auxiliary models, the encoder <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e:\mathbb{R}^{s}\times\{0,1\}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e:\mathbb{R}^{s}\times\{0,1\}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> that embeds the binary message <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> into the representation of the input object <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the decoder <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d:\mathbb{R}^{k}\to\{0,1\}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d:\mathbb{R}^{k}\to\{0,1\}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> that extracts a binary message from the output embedding of the VFM. Given the input image <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the source model <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and the message <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> embedded into <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the goal of the method is two-fold: on the one hand, the decoder <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> should extract close messages from the representations <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for the model <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}\sim f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}\sim f</annotation></semantics></math>; on the other hand, given the model <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\perp f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⟂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\perp f</annotation></semantics></math>, the messages extracted from the representations <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)</annotation></semantics></math> have to be far apart.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The formal problem statement goes as follows. Given <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> as the secret input image used for watermarking, a predefined threshold <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\ll n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\ll n</annotation></semantics></math> and probability thresholds <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\gamma_{1}\ll\gamma_{2}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\gamma_{1}\ll\gamma_{2}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the following inequalities should hold:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\left(\|w-d(f^{\prime}(x))\|_{1}\leq\tau\right)\geq\gamma_{1},\\
\\
\mathbb{P}\left(\|w-d(g(x))\|_{1}\leq\tau\right)\leq\gamma_{2},\\
\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\left(\|w-d(f^{\prime}(x))\|_{1}\leq\tau\right)\geq\gamma_{1},\\
\\
\mathbb{P}\left(\|w-d(g(x))\|_{1}\leq\tau\right)\leq\gamma_{2},\\
\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=e(x,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=e(x,m)</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding with the watermark, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}\sim f,\ g\perp f." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⟂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}\sim f,\ g\perp f.</annotation></semantics></math> In Eq. <a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 3.1 Problem Statement ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the probabilities are taken over the randomness induced by the encoder; this randomness will be discussed in the subsequent sections.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Threat Model</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we discuss the conditions under which the proposed method is expected to operate correctly and outline the potential adversary’s capabilities. The goal of an adversary is to remove an existing watermark from a model so that ownership cannot be verified. Specifically, an adversary may attempt either a watermark removal attack, aiming to eliminate the watermark while preserving the model’s functionality, or a model extraction attack, trying to obtain a copy of the watermarked model without the watermark. Possible attacks include fine-tuning the model on downstream tasks or pruning. The objective of the watermarking method is to reliably determine whether a suspect model is a functional copy of the watermarked visual foundation model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Proposed Method</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce RandMark, a novel watermarking approach designed for visual foundation models. RandMark embeds user-specific binary signatures into the representations of a randomly transformed set of input images. To do so, we fine-tune the source model together with the lightweight encoder and decoder networks. This approach enables ownership verification by extracting digital fingerprints from the set of randomly transformed specific set of input images and computing the statistic of resulting random variables.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The watermarking process goes as follows. First of all, given input image <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and user-specific binary message <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,</annotation></semantics></math> we inject <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> into the representation of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+\varepsilon_{j},\ \varepsilon_{j}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}(x)I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+\varepsilon_{j},\ \varepsilon_{j}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}(x)I)</annotation></semantics></math> by training the small encoder <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> and fine-tuning the source model <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. Modified representation, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x+\varepsilon_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x+\varepsilon_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, is then passed to the decoder network <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> that extracts binary message <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> from it. We highlight that the extracted messages, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, are random variables due to the randomness in transformation of input image.
The encoder, decoder, and the source foundation model are trained jointly to minimize both the average discrepancy between <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and the variance of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}_{j}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}_{j}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Loss function</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The training objective is the combination of two terms: given the input sample <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the first one ensures that the feature representations of the watermarked and original models do not deviate much; the second term forces the extracted binary messages to be close to the embedded one. Specifically, the objective function is</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(x,f,\tilde{f})=\|f(x)-\tilde{f}(x)\|_{2}+\frac{\lambda}{K}\sum_{j=1}^{K}\|m-m_{j}^{\prime}\|_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(x,f,\tilde{f})=\|f(x)-\tilde{f}(x)\|_{2}+\frac{\lambda}{K}\sum_{j=1}^{K}\|m-m_{j}^{\prime}\|_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a scalar parameter, <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is the watermarked version of <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{j}^{\prime}=d(\tilde{f}(e(x+\varepsilon_{j},m)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{j}^{\prime}=d(\tilde{f}(e(x+\varepsilon_{j},m)))</annotation></semantics></math> is the binary message extracted by the decoder from <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+\varepsilon_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+\varepsilon_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of transformations of the input image. This formulation ensures the successful embedding and extraction of watermarks with little to no impact on the feature representation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Evaluating the efficiency of the method</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the performance of the proposed method, given the user-specific watermark <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and input image <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute both the sample average and the sample variance of the variable <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|m-m^{\prime}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|m-m^{\prime}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the watermark. Here we recall that the extracted watermarks are random.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Namely, if the total number of transformations of the input image is <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and the length of the watermark is <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,
we measure the average number of matching bits between <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in the form</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{E}}\|m-m^{\prime}\|_{1}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{j=1}^{K}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathds{1}\left(m_{i}\neq(m^{\prime}_{j})_{i}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{E}}\|m-m^{\prime}\|_{1}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{j=1}^{K}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathds{1}\left(m_{i}\neq(m^{\prime}_{j})_{i}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and the sample variance is computed as</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{V}}\|m-m^{\prime}\|_{1}=\frac{1}{K-1}\sum_{j=1}^{K}\left(d_{j}-\hat{\mathbb{E}}\|m-m^{\prime}\|_{1}\right)^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{V}}\|m-m^{\prime}\|_{1}=\frac{1}{K-1}\sum_{j=1}^{K}\left(d_{j}-\hat{\mathbb{E}}\|m-m^{\prime}\|_{1}\right)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{j}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathds{1}\left(m_{i}\neq(m^{\prime}_{j})_{i}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{j}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathds{1}\left(m_{i}\neq(m^{\prime}_{j})_{i}\right),</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{j}^{\prime}=d(\tilde{f}(e(x+\varepsilon_{j},m)))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{j}^{\prime}=d(\tilde{f}(e(x+\varepsilon_{j},m))).</annotation></semantics></math> The intuition behind using this two metrics is as follows. First of all, given the extracted message <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime},</annotation></semantics></math> the distance from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ Evaluating the efficiency of the method ‣ 3.3 Proposed Method ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is expected to be small for the watermarked model and large for an independent model. Secondly, if we introduce an auxiliary variable in the form</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v(f,h)=\mathbb{V}\left(\|m^{\prime}(f)-m^{\prime}(h)\|_{1}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v(f,h)=\mathbb{V}\left(\|m^{\prime}(f)-m^{\prime}(h)\|_{1}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">then <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(f,f^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(f,f^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is expected to be small for <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}\sim f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}\sim f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> is expected to be large for <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\perp f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⟂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\perp f</annotation></semantics></math>. We elaborate on this point in the subsequent sections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, the decision rule that is used to evaluate whether the given network is watermarked is the comparison of the distance with a predefined threshold: given the suspicious model <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, input image <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, secret message <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and the series of <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> watermarks <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}_{1},m^{\prime}_{2},\dots,m^{\prime}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}_{1},m^{\prime}_{2},\dots,m^{\prime}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> extracted from <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, we treat <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> as watermarked if</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(x)=\hat{\mathbb{E}}\|m-m^{\prime}\|_{1}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{j=1}^{K}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathds{1}\left(m_{i}\neq(m^{\prime}_{j})_{i}\right)\leq\tau," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(x)=\hat{\mathbb{E}}\|m-m^{\prime}\|_{1}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{j=1}^{K}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathds{1}\left(m_{i}\neq(m^{\prime}_{j})_{i}\right)\leq\tau,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> is the threshold value. In the case of many input images used for watermarking, namely, for <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> images from <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\{x_{1},\dots,x_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\{x_{1},\dots,x_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the performance of the method is illustrated by the watermark detection rate, <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(h,\mathcal{X},\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(h,\mathcal{X},\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, in the form below:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R(h,\mathcal{X},\tau)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{x_{i}\in\mathcal{X}}\mathds{1}[\rho(x_{i})\leq\tau]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></munder><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(h,\mathcal{X},\tau)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{x_{i}\in\mathcal{X}}\mathds{1}[\rho(x_{i})\leq\tau].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">As an auxiliary indicator of the model being watermarked, for each <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X},</annotation></semantics></math> we compute the value of statistic <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(f,h)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(f,h).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Setting the threshold value</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We set the threshold by formulating a hypothesis test: the null hypothesis, <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}=</annotation></semantics></math> “the model <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is not watermarked”, is tested against an alternative hypothesis, <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}=</annotation></semantics></math> “the model <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is watermarked”, for the given suspicious model <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. In this section, we assume that the probabilities that the <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>th bit in <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}(h)</annotation></semantics></math> coincide are the same for all <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[1,n]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[1,n].</annotation></semantics></math> Having said so, we estimate the probability of false acceptance of hypothesis <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (namely, <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FPR_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FPR_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>) as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="FPR_{1}=\mathbb{P}_{g\sim\Xi}[\rho(m,m^{\prime}(g,x))&lt;\tau]=\sum_{j=0}^{\tau}\binom{n}{j}(1-r)^{j}r^{n-j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>τ</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FPR_{1}=\mathbb{P}_{g\sim\Xi}[\rho(m,m^{\prime}(g,x))&lt;\tau]=\sum_{j=0}^{\tau}\binom{n}{j}(1-r)^{j}r^{n-j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\mathbb{P}_{g\sim\Xi}(m_{i}=m^{\prime}(g,x)_{i})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\mathbb{P}_{g\sim\Xi}(m_{i}=m^{\prime}(g,x)_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math>
To choose a proper threshold value for <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, we set up an upper bound for <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FPR_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FPR_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and solve for <math id="S3.SS3.SSSx3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, namely,</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\arg\max_{\tau^{\prime}&lt;n}\left(\sum_{j=0}^{\tau^{\prime}}\binom{n}{j}(1-r)^{j}r^{n-j}\right)\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\sum_{j=0}^{\tau^{\prime}}\binom{n}{j}(1-r)^{j}r^{n-j}&lt;\varepsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\arg\max_{\tau^{\prime}&lt;n}\left(\sum_{j=0}^{\tau^{\prime}}\binom{n}{j}(1-r)^{j}r^{n-j}\right)\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\sum_{j=0}^{\tau^{\prime}}\binom{n}{j}(1-r)^{j}r^{n-j}&lt;\varepsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Difference between watermarked and non-watermarked models</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that a good watermarking approach should yield a high watermark detection rate from (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Evaluating the efficiency of the method ‣ 3.3 Proposed Method ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) for the models that are functionally connected to the watermarked one and, at the same time, low detection rates for independent models. To assess the integrity of the proposed approach, we estimate the probabilities of the method to yield low detection rates for functionally dependent models and high detection rates for independent models in the form</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{f^{\prime}\sim\Omega_{f}}[R(f^{\prime},\mathcal{X},\tau)&lt;\overline{R}],\quad\mathbb{P}_{g\sim\Xi}[R(g,\mathcal{X},\tau)&gt;\underline{R}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{f^{\prime}\sim\Omega_{f}}[R(f^{\prime},\mathcal{X},\tau)&lt;\overline{R}],\quad\mathbb{P}_{g\sim\Xi}[R(g,\mathcal{X},\tau)&gt;\underline{R}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for some threshold values <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\underline{R}&lt;\overline{R}&lt;N." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>&lt;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\underline{R}&lt;\overline{R}&lt;N.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To estimate the probabilities from (<a href="#S3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 3.4 Difference between watermarked and non-watermarked models ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), we firstly provide one-sided interval estimations for conditional probabilities of bit collisions in the form</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r(\Omega_{f}|x)=\mathbb{P}_{f^{\prime}\sim\Omega_{f}}[m_{i}=m^{\prime}(f^{\prime},x)_{i}],\quad r(\Xi|x)=\mathbb{P}_{g\sim\Xi}[m_{i}=m^{\prime}(g,x)_{i}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\Omega_{f}|x)=\mathbb{P}_{f^{\prime}\sim\Omega_{f}}[m_{i}=m^{\prime}(f^{\prime},x)_{i}],\quad r(\Xi|x)=\mathbb{P}_{g\sim\Xi}[m_{i}=m^{\prime}(g,x)_{i}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We do it by sampling <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> functionally dependent models, namely, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}^{\prime},\dots,f^{\prime}_{M}\sim\Omega_{f}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}^{\prime},\dots,f^{\prime}_{M}\sim\Omega_{f},</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> independent models, namely <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1},\dots,g_{M}\sim\Xi." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1},\dots,g_{M}\sim\Xi.</annotation></semantics></math> Here, the space <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math> of independent models consists of visual foundation models, both of the same architecture and of different architectures as <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, by either fine-tuning of <em id="S3.SS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-watermarked</em> copy of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> for a downstream task, of via functionality stealing perturbations, for example, via knowledge distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> or pruning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Similarly, the space <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the models, both of the same architecture and of different architectures as <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, by either fine-tuning of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> for a downstream task, of via functionality stealing perturbations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then, given the set <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\{x_{1},\dots,x_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\{x_{1},\dots,x_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of images used for the watermarking of <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> from (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Evaluating the efficiency of the method ‣ 3.3 Proposed Method ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), we compute the quantities</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathds{1}(f^{\prime}_{j},i,x_{l})=\mathds{1}[m_{i}=m^{\prime}(f^{\prime}_{j},x_{l})_{i}]\quad\text{and}\quad\mathds{1}(g_{j},i,x_{l})=\mathds{1}[m_{i}=m^{\prime}(g_{j},x_{l})_{i}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mtext>and</mtext><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathds{1}(f^{\prime}_{j},i,x_{l})=\mathds{1}[m_{i}=m^{\prime}(f^{\prime}_{j},x_{l})_{i}]\quad\text{and}\quad\mathds{1}(g_{j},i,x_{l})=\mathds{1}[m_{i}=m^{\prime}(g_{j},x_{l})_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}\mathbb{P}(r(\Omega_{f}|x)&lt;l(x))\leq\frac{\alpha}{N},\\
\mathbb{P}(r(\Xi|x)&gt;u(x))\leq\frac{\alpha}{N}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}\mathbb{P}(r(\Omega_{f}|x)&lt;l(x))\leq\frac{\alpha}{N},\\
\mathbb{P}(r(\Xi|x)&gt;u(x))\leq\frac{\alpha}{N}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">These estimates, namely, <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, are used to estimate the probabilities from (<a href="#S3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 3.4 Difference between watermarked and non-watermarked models ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Estimating the probability of a deviation of the detection rate</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we discuss how to upper-bound both the probability of false detection of a non-watermarked model as a copy of the watermarked one and the probability of misdetecting a functional copy of the watermarked model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(f^{\prime},\mathcal{X},\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(f^{\prime},\mathcal{X},\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum of <math id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> independent Bernoulli variables with parameters, <math id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\Omega_{f}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\Omega_{f}|x)</annotation></semantics></math>, so</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{f^{\prime}\sim\Omega_{f}}[R(f^{\prime},\mathcal{X},\tau)&lt;\overline{R}]=\sum_{l=0}^{\overline{R}-1}\sum_{S\subset\mathcal{X}:|S|=l}\prod_{x_{in}\in S}r(\Omega_{f}|x_{in})\prod_{x_{out}\notin S}(1-r(\Omega_{f}|x_{out}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{f^{\prime}\sim\Omega_{f}}[R(f^{\prime},\mathcal{X},\tau)&lt;\overline{R}]=\sum_{l=0}^{\overline{R}-1}\sum_{S\subset\mathcal{X}:|S|=l}\prod_{x_{in}\in S}r(\Omega_{f}|x_{in})\prod_{x_{out}\notin S}(1-r(\Omega_{f}|x_{out})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Note that replacing the parameters <math id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\Omega_{f}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\Omega_{f}|x)</annotation></semantics></math> with its estimations in the form <math id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l(x)</annotation></semantics></math> from (<a href="#S3.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ 3.4 Difference between watermarked and non-watermarked models ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) yields the bound</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{f^{\prime}\sim\Omega_{f}}[R(f^{\prime},\mathcal{X},\tau)&lt;\overline{R}]&lt;\sum_{l=0}^{\overline{R}-1}\sum_{S\subset\mathcal{X}:|S|=l}\prod_{x_{in}\in S}l(x_{in})\prod_{x_{out}\notin S}(1-l(x_{out}))=\underline{p}(\Omega)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">&lt;</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{f^{\prime}\sim\Omega_{f}}[R(f^{\prime},\mathcal{X},\tau)&lt;\overline{R}]&lt;\sum_{l=0}^{\overline{R}-1}\sum_{S\subset\mathcal{X}:|S|=l}\prod_{x_{in}\in S}l(x_{in})\prod_{x_{out}\notin S}(1-l(x_{out}))=\underline{p}(\Omega),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">that holds with probability at least <math id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\alpha." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\alpha.</annotation></semantics></math> Similarly,</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{g\sim\Xi}[R(g,\mathcal{X},\tau)&gt;\underline{R}]&lt;\sum_{l=\underline{R}+1}^{N}\sum_{S\subset\mathcal{X}:|S|=l}\prod_{x_{in}\in S}u(x_{in})\prod_{x_{out}\notin S}(1-u(x_{out}))=\underline{p}(\Xi)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">&lt;</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{g\sim\Xi}[R(g,\mathcal{X},\tau)&gt;\underline{R}]&lt;\sum_{l=\underline{R}+1}^{N}\sum_{S\subset\mathcal{X}:|S|=l}\prod_{x_{in}\in S}u(x_{in})\prod_{x_{out}\notin S}(1-u(x_{out}))=\underline{p}(\Xi).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During experimentally, we used <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=32,\tau=5,M=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=32,\tau=5,M=1000</annotation></semantics></math> and varied confidence level <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> such that probabilities <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\underline{p}(\Xi),\underline{p}(\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\underline{p}(\Xi),\underline{p}(\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math> were close. Specifically, value <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=5\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=5\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> yields <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{p}(\Omega)=10^{-6},\underline{p}(\Xi)=10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{p}(\Omega)=10^{-6},\underline{p}(\Xi)=10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{R}=750,\underline{R}=600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>750</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{R}=750,\underline{R}=600</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, if one uses the boundary values <math id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{R},\overline{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{R},\overline{R}</annotation></semantics></math> to distinguish between the watermarked and non-watermarked model, one is guaranteed to have both error probabilities <math id="S3.SS4.SSSx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{p}(\Omega),\underline{p}(\Xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{p}(\Omega),\underline{p}(\Xi)</annotation></semantics></math> low.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Alternative estimation of bit collisions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">According to equation <a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Evaluating the efficiency of the method ‣ 3.3 Proposed Method ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, the quantity <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R(f,\mathcal{X},\tau)=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathds{1}[\rho(m(x_{i}),m^{\prime}(f,x_{i}))\leq\tau]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(f,\mathcal{X},\tau)=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathds{1}[\rho(m(x_{i}),m^{\prime}(f,x_{i}))\leq\tau]</annotation></semantics></math> is the sum of <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> independent Bernoulli random variables. We may rewrite <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}=R(f^{\prime},\mathcal{X},\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}=R(f^{\prime},\mathcal{X},\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}=R(g,\mathcal{X},\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}=R(g,\mathcal{X},\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> from equation <a href="#S3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 3.4 Difference between watermarked and non-watermarked models ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> in the form</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R_{1}=\xi_{1}+\xi_{2}+\dots+\xi_{n-1}+\xi_{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R_{1}=\xi_{1}+\xi_{2}+\dots+\xi_{n-1}+\xi_{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R_{2}=\eta_{1}+\eta_{2}+\dots+\eta_{n-1}+\eta_{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R_{2}=\eta_{1}+\eta_{2}+\dots+\eta_{n-1}+\eta_{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}\sim\emph{Bernoulli}(p_{i}),\eta_{i}\sim\emph{Bernoulli}(q_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}\sim\emph{Bernoulli}(p_{i}),\eta_{i}\sim\emph{Bernoulli}(q_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are independent and parameters <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{i},q_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{i},q_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are unknown. Let <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{p}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{p}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{q}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{q}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, if <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{R}&lt;n\overline{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{R}&lt;n\overline{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{R}&gt;n\overline{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{R}&gt;n\overline{q}</annotation></semantics></math> from equation <a href="#S3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 3.4 Difference between watermarked and non-watermarked models ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, the following lemma holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and set <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2n}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2n}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}=\frac{1}{n}R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}=\frac{1}{n}R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}=\frac{1}{n}R_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}=\frac{1}{n}R_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be unbiased estimates of <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{q}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{q},</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Then, with probability at least <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta,</annotation></semantics></math> the following upper bounds for probabilities from equation <a href="#S3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 3.4 Difference between watermarked and non-watermarked models ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> hold:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}_{f^{\prime}\sim\Omega_{f}}[R(f^{\prime},\mathcal{X},\tau)&lt;\overline{R}]\leq h(\hat{p},\varepsilon^{-})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}_{f^{\prime}\sim\Omega_{f}}[R(f^{\prime},\mathcal{X},\tau)&lt;\overline{R}]\leq h(\hat{p},\varepsilon^{-}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}_{g\sim\Xi}[R(g,\mathcal{X},\tau)&gt;\underline{R}]\leq h(\hat{q},\varepsilon^{+})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}_{g\sim\Xi}[R(g,\mathcal{X},\tau)&gt;\underline{R}]\leq h(\hat{q},\varepsilon^{+}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where</span><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h(\hat{p},\varepsilon^{-})=\left(\frac{n(\hat{p}-\varepsilon)}{\overline{R}}\right)^{\overline{R}}\left(\frac{n(1-(\hat{p}-\varepsilon))}{n-\overline{R}}\right)^{n-\overline{R}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h(\hat{p},\varepsilon^{-})=\left(\frac{n(\hat{p}-\varepsilon)}{\overline{R}}\right)^{\overline{R}}\left(\frac{n(1-(\hat{p}-\varepsilon))}{n-\overline{R}}\right)^{n-\overline{R}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h(\hat{p},\varepsilon^{+})=\left(\frac{n(\hat{p}+\varepsilon)}{\underline{R}}\right)^{\underline{R}}\left(\frac{n(1-(\hat{p}+\varepsilon))}{n-\underline{R}}\right)^{n-\underline{R}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h(\hat{p},\varepsilon^{+})=\left(\frac{n(\hat{p}+\varepsilon)}{\underline{R}}\right)^{\underline{R}}\left(\frac{n(1-(\hat{p}+\varepsilon))}{n-\underline{R}}\right)^{n-\underline{R}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Some words about relation <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{p};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{p};</annotation></semantics></math> proof will be moved to the appendix.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a proof for the upper inequality from equation <a href="#S3.Ex2" title="In Lemma 1 ‣ 3.5 Alternative estimation of bit collisions ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>. Specifically, we need to upper bound the probability <math id="Thmproofx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(R\leq d)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(R\leq d),</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmproofx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\equiv R(f^{\prime},\mathcal{X},\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\equiv R(f^{\prime},\mathcal{X},\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\equiv\overline{R}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≡</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\equiv\overline{R}.</annotation></semantics></math> According to Chernoff bound,</p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(R&lt;d)\leq\inf_{t&lt;0}\exp(-td)\mathbb{E}(\exp(tR))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(R&lt;d)\leq\inf_{t&lt;0}\exp(-td)\mathbb{E}(\exp(tR)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproofx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that, according to independence of <math id="Thmproofx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S3.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}(\exp(tR))=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\mathbb{E}(\exp t\xi_{i})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}(1-p_{i}+p_{i}e^{t}),\ \text{and, hence,}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mtext>and, hence,</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}(\exp(tR))=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\mathbb{E}(\exp t\xi_{i})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}(1-p_{i}+p_{i}e^{t}),\ \text{and, hence,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(R&lt;d)\leq\inf_{t&lt;0}\left[\exp(-td)\prod_{i=1}^{n}(1-p_{i}+p_{i}e^{t})\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(R&lt;d)\leq\inf_{t&lt;0}\left[\exp(-td)\prod_{i=1}^{n}(1-p_{i}+p_{i}e^{t})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmproofx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(p)=\ln(1-p+pe^{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(p)=\ln(1-p+pe^{t}).</annotation></semantics></math> Note that <math id="Thmproofx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime\prime}(p)=-\frac{(e^{t}-1)^{2}}{(1-p+pe^{t})^{2}}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime\prime}(p)=-\frac{(e^{t}-1)^{2}}{(1-p+pe^{t})^{2}}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmproofx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and, hence, <math id="Thmproofx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(p)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly concave on <math id="Thmproofx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From the concavity of <math id="Thmproofx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(p)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(p),</annotation></semantics></math> it follows that</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ln(1-p_{i}+p_{i}e^{t})\leq\ln(1-\overline{p}+\overline{p}e^{t})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ln(1-p_{i}+p_{i}e^{t})\leq\ln(1-\overline{p}+\overline{p}e^{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\prod_{i=1}^{n}\left[1-p_{i}+p_{i}e^{t}\right]\leq(1-\overline{p}+\overline{p}e^{t})^{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\prod_{i=1}^{n}\left[1-p_{i}+p_{i}e^{t}\right]\leq(1-\overline{p}+\overline{p}e^{t})^{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproofx1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and, consequently,</p>
<table id="S3.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(R&lt;d)\leq\inf_{t&lt;0}\left[\exp(-td)\left(1-\overline{p}+\overline{p}e^{t}\right)^{n}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(R&lt;d)\leq\inf_{t&lt;0}\left[\exp(-td)\left(1-\overline{p}+\overline{p}e^{t}\right)^{n}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Denote <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(t)=\exp(-td)(1-p+pe^{t})^{n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(t)=\exp(-td)(1-p+pe^{t})^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math> To find <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{t&lt;0}\psi(t)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{t&lt;0}\psi(t),</annotation></semantics></math> we analyze the derivatives of its logarithm:</p>
<table id="S3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}\ln\psi(t)=-d+\frac{npe^{t}}{1-p+pe^{t}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}\ln\psi(t)=-d+\frac{npe^{t}}{1-p+pe^{t}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproofx1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}\ln\psi(t)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}\ln\psi(t)=0</annotation></semantics></math> iff <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-d+\frac{npy}{1-p+py}=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-d+\frac{npy}{1-p+py}=0,</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=e^{t}&lt;1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=e^{t}&lt;1.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S3.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=-d+\frac{npy}{1-p+py}\leftrightarrow y=\frac{d-pd}{np-pd}\leftrightarrow t=\ln\left(\frac{d-pd}{np-pd}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=-d+\frac{npy}{1-p+py}\leftrightarrow y=\frac{d-pd}{np-pd}\leftrightarrow t=\ln\left(\frac{d-pd}{np-pd}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproofx1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">To satisfy <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y&lt;1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&lt;1,</annotation></semantics></math> it is required that <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&lt;np." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&lt;np.</annotation></semantics></math>
Since <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d^{2}}{dt^{2}}\ln\psi(t)=\frac{npe^{t}(1-p)}{(1-p+pe^{t})^{2}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d^{2}}{dt^{2}}\ln\psi(t)=\frac{npe^{t}(1-p)}{(1-p+pe^{t})^{2}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is monotonic, <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\ln\left(\frac{d-pd}{np-pd}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\ln\left(\frac{d-pd}{np-pd}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is a unique critical point of <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}\ln\psi(t)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}\ln\psi(t).</annotation></semantics></math>
Thus,</p>
<table id="S3.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{t&lt;0}\psi(t)=\gamma(p)=\left(\frac{np}{d}\right)^{d}\left(\frac{n(1-p)}{n-d}\right)^{n-d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{t&lt;0}\psi(t)=\gamma(p)=\left(\frac{np}{d}\right)^{d}\left(\frac{n(1-p)}{n-d}\right)^{n-d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproofx1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">and the overall bound is</p>
<table id="S3.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(R&lt;d)\leq\gamma(\overline{p})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(R&lt;d)\leq\gamma(\overline{p})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproofx1.p4.5" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\overline{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\overline{p})</annotation></semantics></math> is impossible to compute directly (since <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{p}</annotation></semantics></math>) is unknown. Instead, we note that <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(p)</annotation></semantics></math> is monotonic in <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p.</annotation></semantics></math>
Indeed,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dp}\ln\gamma(p)=\frac{d}{p}-\frac{n-d}{1-p}\rightarrow\frac{d}{dp}\ln\gamma(p)=0\ \text{at }p=\frac{d}{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>at </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dp}\ln\gamma(p)=\frac{d}{p}-\frac{n-d}{1-p}\rightarrow\frac{d}{dp}\ln\gamma(p)=0\ \text{at }p=\frac{d}{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d^{2}}{dp^{2}}\ln\gamma(p)=-\frac{d}{p^{2}}-\frac{n-d}{(1-p)^{2}}&lt;0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d^{2}}{dp^{2}}\ln\gamma(p)=-\frac{d}{p^{2}}-\frac{n-d}{(1-p)^{2}}&lt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproofx1.p4.6" class="ltx_p">and hence <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(p)</annotation></semantics></math> has a unique global maximum at <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\frac{d}{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\frac{d}{n},</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly increasing on <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,\frac{d}{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,\frac{d}{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and is strictly decreasing on <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\frac{d}{n},1]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\frac{d}{n},1].</annotation></semantics></math>
Recall that <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&lt;np," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&lt;np,</annotation></semantics></math> so <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(p)</annotation></semantics></math> is decreasing in <math id="Thmproofx1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">If we set <math id="Thmproofx1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}=\frac{1}{n}R_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}=\frac{1}{n}R_{1},</annotation></semantics></math> then, according to Hoeffding inequality,</p>
<table id="S3.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(\overline{p}\leq\hat{p}-\varepsilon)\leq\exp(-2\varepsilon^{2}n)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(\overline{p}\leq\hat{p}-\varepsilon)\leq\exp(-2\varepsilon^{2}n)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproofx1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">and from monotonicity of <math id="Thmproofx1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(p)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmproofx1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}-\varepsilon&gt;\frac{d}{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}-\varepsilon&gt;\frac{d}{n},</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S3.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(R&lt;d)\leq\gamma(\hat{p}-\varepsilon)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(R&lt;d)\leq\gamma(\hat{p}-\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproofx1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">with probability at least <math id="Thmproofx1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmproofx1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2n}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2n}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)},</annotation></semantics></math> what concludes the proof. The proof of the second inequality from equation <a href="#S3.Ex2" title="In Lemma 1 ‣ 3.5 Alternative estimation of bit collisions ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> is similar.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted our experiments using two large-scale VFMs, CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> and DINOv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. To train models on downstream tasks (namely, for classification and segmentation), we utilized three domain-specific datasets:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">E-commerce Product Images: This dataset consists of <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18,175" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18,175</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18,175</annotation></semantics></math> product images categorized into <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> major classes based on Amazon’s product taxonomy. It is primarily used for image-based product categorization.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">FoodSeg103: A food image segmentation dataset containing <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7,118" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7,118</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7,118</annotation></semantics></math> images annotated with fine-grained pixel-wise labels for over <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> food categories. It supports both semantic segmentation and instance-level analysis of food items.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Watermark injection</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both source VFMs were initialized with publicly available pretrained weights. To embed watermarks, we use a random subset of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1000</annotation></semantics></math> images from the ImageNet dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, assigning each image a randomly sampled binary vector <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=32</annotation></semantics></math>. The image and its corresponding message are jointly processed by an encoder, and the resulting output is forwarded through the VFM, allowing the watermark information to be incorporated while preserving model functionality. The embedded watermark can later be extracted using the RandMark procedure, producing a binary message <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to verify model ownership. A schematic illustration of the method is presented in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, and detailed architectural descriptions are provided in the Supplementary Material.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Functional perturbations of VFM</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate the robustness of watermarks embedded by RandMark, we evaluate how the detection rate from (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Evaluating the efficiency of the method ‣ 3.3 Proposed Method ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) changes under fine-tuning of the model for downstream tasks and under pruning. Specifically, we fine-tune all the layers of the watermarked VFM for both classification and segmentation downstream tasks, using the aforementioned datasets. The fine-tuning was performed using the AdamW optimizer for <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> epochs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the impact of model sparsity on both classification accuracy and watermark robustness, we applied post-training unstructured <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm pruning to the entire model. We evaluated two sparsity levels: moderate pruning, where 20% of the lowest-magnitude weights were zeroed out, and aggressive pruning, where 40% of the weights were removed. This procedure enabled us to assess the effect of varying sparsity levels on watermark reconstruction. Note that the unrestricted <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm pruning is used purely as the baseline to illustrate the robustness of the proposed method to the modifications of the model.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Results</h2>

<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10695v1/clip_figure.svg" id="S5.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:219/298;" width="219" height="298"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10695v1/dino_figure.svg" id="S5.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:219/298;" width="219" height="298"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Watermark detection rate <math id="S5.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> from (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Evaluating the efficiency of the method ‣ 3.3 Proposed Method ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), averaged over <math id="S5.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1000</annotation></semantics></math> images used for watermarking. Classification experiments were conducted on the E-commerce Product Images dataset, segmentation experiment was conducted on the FoodSeg103 dataset. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10695v1/dino_covariance.svg" id="S5.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/191;" width="476" height="191"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The architecture of VFM is DINOv2</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10695v1/clip_covariance.svg" id="S5.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/191;" width="476" height="191"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The architecture of VFM is CLIP</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution of covariance between decoded watermark messages from two models <math id="S5.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Independent models produce covariance values near zero, while watermark-dependent models exhibit positive covariance due to correlated decoding of watermark bits.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experimentally, we assess the efficiency of RandMark  by computing the average watermark detection rate from (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Evaluating the efficiency of the method ‣ 3.3 Proposed Method ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) for different values of maximum number of bit errors, <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau.</annotation></semantics></math> In Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5 Results ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we demonstrate that our method can be used to reliably detect models that are functionally connected to the watermarked one, namely, the ones obtained via fine-tuning for downstream tasks and pruning. At the same time, RandMark   does not falsely detect the presence of the watermarks in negative suspect models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the detection rate, we analyze correlations between decoded watermark messages. Let <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> denote two models, and let <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}(g)</annotation></semantics></math> be the watermark bit sequences decoded from them. The covariance between the decoded messages is estimated as</p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\frac{\mathbb{V}(m^{\prime}(f))+\mathbb{V}(m^{\prime}(g))-\mathbb{V}(m^{\prime}(f)-m^{\prime}(g))}{2}=\text{cov}(f,g)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>cov</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\frac{\mathbb{V}(m^{\prime}(f))+\mathbb{V}(m^{\prime}(g))-\mathbb{V}(m^{\prime}(f)-m^{\prime}(g))}{2}=\text{cov}(f,g).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In Fig. <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5 Results ‣ RandMark: On Random Watermarking of Visual Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for independent models, <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is close to zero, indicating negligible correlation. In contrast, watermark-dependent models exhibit positive covariance, reflecting correlated decoding of watermark bits. This complementary metric provides additional evidence of functional dependence between models and can help distinguish watermarked models from unrelated ones.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Comparison with the baseline fingerprinting and watermarking approaches</h3>

<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison with baseline watermarking methods under segmentation fine-tuning.
Segmentation performance and watermark extraction accuracy are reported after different numbers of fine-tuning epochs; the architecture of the source VFM is CLIP.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">1 epoch</th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">3 epochs</th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">5 epochs</th></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row">Method</th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">Segm. <math id="S5.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">WM <math id="S5.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">Segm. <math id="S5.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">WM <math id="S5.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">Segm. <math id="S5.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">WM <math id="S5.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
